WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:01.319 --> 00:00:08.460
Thank you.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:16.760
This draws directly on from David's presentation,
where he talked about the Call to Action.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.180
One of our prime duties in the legislation
of course is to promote a comprehensive NHS,

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.830
and that is not just for now, that is into
the future.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:32.029
That is what our strategy process is about.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:36.660
I will not go through the paper in detail,
nor the Call to Action itself, because I think

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:43.120
when we launched it last Thursday it got a
good deal of prominence and, indeed, quite

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.090
a lot of support for its approach.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:49.430
I do want to just pick out four points.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:55.039
Inaction
The message at the heart of the Call to Action

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:59.570
analysis is that doing nothing is not an option.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:07.159
I know the Institute for Fiscal Studies have
just published their latest report on the

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:11.960
economic outlook, and they are reinforcing
some of the messages in the Call to Action

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:18.439
about the state of public finances going forward,
that they are not going to suddenly open up

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:22.799
and become much easier, much more generous.

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:28.110
I think in a number of the debates we have
heard around that and around other long term

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:36.299
economic projections, people have identified
a set of trade-offs for public services.

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:39.369
Are we going to look to reduce entitlements?

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:43.270
Are we going to look to increase charging?

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:48.530
Are we prepared to drop standards in particular
areas?

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:55.619
NHS England in its Call to Action is saying
none of those are acceptable options to us;

00:01:55.619 --> 00:02:01.380
they do not fit with our values, they do not
fit with our mission for serving patients

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.110
and population in this country.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:10.740
So, that leaves us in a place described by
this piece of work, which is: we need to be

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:15.820
able to identify the right changes in the
interests of patients, in the interests of

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:21.440
communities, and be able to bring about those
changes at an accelerated rate.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.440
Methodology
That leads, I think, to the second point I

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.920
would draw out, which is how you would go
about that, and I think that is only through

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.610
a process of really honest, open engagement
and involvement.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:40.180
So, while we have set out the analysis last
Thursday, that is just the start of a process

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:45.230
we want to see right the way across the country,
where our partners in health and wellbeing

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:52.860
boards, where patients, the public, voluntary
groups and charitable groups are all deeply

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.960
involved in sharing with us their understanding
of what great health systems should be looking

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.210
like over the next five to ten years.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:06.050
It is through that process of involvement
and engagement that I think we will be able

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:12.100
then to respond more swiftly maybe than the
health system has been able to do previously.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.940
Support
I think the third point that gives me quite

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.470
a lot of confidence in this is the nature
of support that we have had from many of our

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:21.550
partners nationally.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:28.570
So, I know in the paper which we wrote a little
bit before we published the Call to Action

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:35.950
itself, we name check a number of our national
partners; in the final tally, every single

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.700
one of the health system partners signed up.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:48.590
They wanted their names to be associated with
this work, but also local governments represented

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:53.170
by local government association and I think,
really importantly, the clinical commissioning

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.600
groups across the country through their representatives.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:05.350
So, this is a very broadly owned mission to
identify a vision for the NHS in five to ten

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:06.350
years.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.940
Future
This leads to I think my last point, which

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:19.780
is that this will not be just about interesting
ideas and good conversations; this will lead

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:26.110
to planning, and we will a traction which
will help us actually deliver those changes.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:31.170
That is both at the level of clinical commissioning
groups and health and wellbeing boards where

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:38.050
we will be looking through the end of this
to come up with real plans for change.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:44.441
Similarly, for us as the NHS England commissioner
for the way we commission primary care, for

00:04:44.441 --> 00:04:50.920
the way we commission specialised services,
for the whole range of work we can do around

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:58.190
incentives and financial change, but also
in the area of transparency and that whole

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:04.570
relationship between citizens and service
that Tim and his team, I think, are leading

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.610
in really quite an innovative and exciting
way.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:13.370
We will be setting out real plans for how
we make that real over the next five years.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:19.190
Therefore, I would commend this to the Board,
and I would note that this is just the start

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.970
of a very significant process of engagement
over the coming months.

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:26.440
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Bill.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:31.740
I think all the Board recognises that this
is essential but that also it will be a very

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:32.950
tough exercise.

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:40.110
We have got a very good consensus around us
for the case for change.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.740
Maintaining that consensus as we go through
to examine new options is going to be a real

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:44.740
challenge.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:50.060
It is going to be essential, I think, that
we engage clinicians and front-line delivery

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:57.830
people right across the NHS to help us to
envisage this because it will affect how the

00:05:57.830 --> 00:05:59.680
whole service looks in 10 years.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.501
It will affect what we can do for the public.

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:03.610
It will affect the empowerment of patients.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:09.050
There is a huge emphasis on prevention and
the public health agenda that is all tied

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.020
in together.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.889
What it does most of all is disrupt the existing
set of expectations.

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:18.170
It is not a structural change.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:22.870
We have to work within existing structures,
but we also have to be able -- as a Board,

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:29.710
I think -- to ensure that there is sufficient
commitment and stability over time, so that

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:36.460
people can see a direction of travel, a convergence
of support around that, if we are to have

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:42.240
any chance of maintaining the highest quality
healthcare system in these adverse financial

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:43.650
circumstances.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:49.360
Let me invite comments, if I may, from members
of the Board.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.440
Moira Gibb: Malcolm, thank you.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:58.130
I think David made reference to other healthcare
systems, as it were.

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:04.160
Is there going to be an international perspective
to this work, and are there examples?

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.639
Often when the NHS is discussed, it is terms
of not comparing well.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:12.120
However, I think it compares very well.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:18.880
Are there examples of what has happened in
other places that we will bring into this

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:19.880
discussion?

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:26.130
Personally, I think it is quite difficult
to consider myself as a patient in these circumstances

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:33.070
rather than in any other capacity, and to
think differently unless you can see examples.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:36.980
Giving people opportunities to see what is
happening and how things have changed elsewhere

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:37.980
might be very valuable.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:41.220
Bill McCarthy: I think that is absolutely
right, Moira.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:46.470
I have two points on that.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:53.360
I think probably the most respected think
tank who compare different health systems

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.550
is the Commonwealth Foundation, which is based
in Washington.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:03.710
They take a number of the developed nations.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:10.949
They look at the health systems and they try
to compare against a set of criteria: efficiency;

00:08:10.949 --> 00:08:14.280
effectiveness; quality; outcomes.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.930
Consistently, the NHS rates very highly.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:23.950
It has been in the top two in the past three
comparisons they have done.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.720
To an extent, that is great news for us.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:33.229
That really does validate us on the excellence
across the NHS.

00:08:33.229 --> 00:08:38.790
However, we do know that there are elements
where we can learn.

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:44.870
A Lancet article several months ago compared
some of the outcomes in particular clinical

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.780
areas where we can improve and we must improve.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:55.300
As well as feeding in some of that data and
analysis, we are proposing this autumn to

00:08:55.300 --> 00:09:03.210
bring together what we are calling a symposium
of some of the expert think tanks and some

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:08.220
other organisations who have experience of
working both in this country and abroad, and

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:14.200
bringing them together into one place to describe
-- from that experience and learning -- what

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:21.290
they think a great NHS might look like in
10 years' time.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:25.930
We think just bringing some of that thought
leadership and this exchange of ideas would

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:31.940
be a really good way to challenge us at the
time that we are doing our own planning.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:36.400
Naguib Kheraj: I have just two comments, Bill.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.500
I thought the document was excellent.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:47.020
I think that setting out the really big things
that need to be addressed to make the whole

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.680
equation work is really critical.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:54.100
There are two things I wanted to make a point
about.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:57.850
The first is: how do you translate this?

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:01.360
You talked about the things we may do over
the coming month.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:09.210
The concern I have is that we have a debate
about the long term but we do not have actions

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:14.580
that we are taking that take us on a path
for the long term.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:19.820
Over the course of the next six months, what
will actually do with this?

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:24.010
The second point is related to that.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:31.100
If we now look at every decision we are taking
that is incremental -- for example, if we

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:40.250
are looking at next year's plans in different
areas -- how do we already start to take on

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.600
Board the direction of travel in the decisions
we make?

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.480
That is something I am struggling with.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:54.390
Bill McCarthy: I think I have probably got
the same answer to both of those questions,

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.380
Naguib.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:11:02.550
I will be quite cartoonish in this because
it would not be fair everywhere but at times

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:06.920
in the NHS we have planned very incrementally.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:13.830
We have thought how things are now, what the
next year's money will look like, how we can

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:19.330
make some improvements, keep our heads above
water, and we are always looking at that short

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:21.050
term.

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:27.370
The idea here is that we put that planning
into the context of where we want to be in

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:31.420
five to 10 years' time.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:37.220
That will only really make a difference if
we connect it back into our planning processes.

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:42.210
While we will have the vision, we will have
the challenge and we will have the question

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:48.610
of how services should look five to 10 years
out, we will follow that up with the proper

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:51.540
planning cycle.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:55.860
We will be working with people to say, 'if
that is where you want to be, what are the

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.620
steps you are taking next year, and what are
the steps you plan to take two years out?'

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:09.240
We would expect pretty hard, specific actions,
backed up by the commissioning intentions

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:16.010
and by the sorts of collaborations with partners
that will be needed to bring about the change.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:22.709
This is not just about a theory of vision
some way it out.

00:12:22.709 --> 00:12:29.480
It provides the context against the really
specific planning to get there over the next

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:30.480
few years.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:36.930
Naguib Kheraj: With that course of action,
the two things that strike me are that usually

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:44.640
when you make big changes either you have
a combination of having to spend more in the

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:51.270
short term to create the change that enables
you to do something differently, or in order

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:58.820
to do something differently or shift resources,
you have to do less of something else.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:02.480
Do you have concerns about those two things?

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:09.750
Is there an investment to create the capacity
or a reduction in something in order to do

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:10.750
it?

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:16.779
Bill McCarthy: Paul may wish to comment, but
I would be very surprised if that strand of

00:13:16.779 --> 00:13:28.410
work, which we have called Financial Incentives
for Change, did not get feedback both nationally

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:33.730
and also our local partners which says, 'we
are not sure they the right incentives and

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:38.230
the right mechanisms to promote and support
change at the pace we need.'

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:43.060
I would have thought that is an area in which
we would need to be quite innovative.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:47.149
Paul Baumann: I think I would just make two
links for you, Naguib.

00:13:47.149 --> 00:13:52.709
One is to the incentives and business model
strands that we are doing, where we will really

00:13:52.709 --> 00:13:56.490
give some thought to the specific question
you ask, which is: what is the transitional

00:13:56.490 --> 00:14:01.270
cost to get from where we are today to where
we want to be, and how do we make the funds

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:03.180
flow to do that?

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.540
Much more concretely, and without wishing
to bring forward an agenda item that comes

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:11.880
later in the meeting, there is the response
to the Spending Review which, in my mind,

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:16.120
has brought forward very much the very focused
discussion we need to have about how we make

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:20.620
that transition, how we use investment money
and -- first of all -- how we create investment

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:21.750
money.

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:29.339
If I can just signal forward to that agenda
item, I will directly address the topic that

00:14:29.339 --> 00:14:32.250
you have raised in that context.

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:35.980
Malcolm Grant: Okay, may we return to Naguib's
point when we reach that item on the agenda,

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:40.930
because the Spending Review is a spur for
carrying out some of this work.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:43.860
Victor Adebowale: I have a question.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:50.839
First of all, I would like to welcome the
fact that you are making a particular effort

00:14:50.839 --> 00:14:55.980
to hear the voices of people who generally
are not heard in consultations like this.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:15:03.430
Naguib's point is that you can stop doing
something, you can do less of something or

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:08.230
you can radically change and redesign something.

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.730
I was just wondering what you think the appetite
is for that, and how we are going to know

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:19.200
that those voices that are not normally heard
are actually influencing the outcome?

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:27.430
Bill McCarthy: My view is that NHS England
absolutely has that appetite and that flows

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:35.540
directly from analysis that tells us that
if we do not pursue some fairly radical redesign

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:40.130
and change in the interests of patients and
communities, and particularly those that have

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:48.070
not perhaps fared so well in the past and
are at risk of being left out, then we get

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:52.930
into that debate which in our values has been
very unpalatable.

00:15:52.930 --> 00:16:00.180
It is absolutely not where we want to go,
which is: what do you pare back on or what

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:02.760
standards are you prepared to compromise on?

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:10.050
I think this is a very clear statement that
we are into that area of compromise and cutting

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:11.050
back.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.110
We are into the area of radical change in
the interest of patients.

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:22.120
Ed Smith: I would just back that up by saying
the whole design of this looking five to 10

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:27.589
years out, and the stakeholder participation
that is absolutely integral to the success

00:16:27.589 --> 00:16:32.560
of this, should enable us to really reimagine
parts of this.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:39.350
It is going beyond redesign to reimagine the
whole construct of how service is delivered,

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:43.779
because the frontline and the consultation
processes will start to bring some of that

00:16:43.779 --> 00:16:44.779
out.

00:16:44.779 --> 00:16:49.820
Victor Adebowale: I think we will find out
what commissioning actually is, rather than

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:51.430
what it has been.

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:55.490
I think it is a very powerful intervention.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:17:00.660
Malcolm Grant: I think that has been very
interesting.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:02.660
Thank you, Bill.

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:06.589
We will expect you to come back to the Board
on several occasions over the coming months

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:13.549
with updates and also real engagement by all
members of the Board, and non-executives as

00:17:13.549 --> 00:17:14.799
much as executives.

00:17:14.799 --> 00:17:17.870
This is something that we all own and that
we all wish to succeed.

00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:18.870
May we then turn to item five on the agenda?

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:19.870
Again, this is another hugely salient issue
for us, which is: the Patient and Public Voice?

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:19.881
I am going to invite Tim Kelsey to introduce
the paper.

